title: Gates in a row
check: behaves
---- intro
Gates can be combined in one line. Brackets say what happens first, as in arithmetic: in `not (a and b)` the AND comes first, and the NOT turns its answer round. That is the NAND you built from two gates.

Without brackets, VHDL makes you be clear: `not a and b` means `(not a) and b`, which is a different circuit.
---- task
Write NAND: `y` is the opposite of `a` AND `b`, in one line.
---- start
library ieee;
use ieee.std_logic_1164.all;

entity nand_gate is
  port(
    a : in  std_logic;
    b : in  std_logic;
    y : out std_logic
  );
end nand_gate;

architecture rtl of nand_gate is
begin
    -- write it here
end rtl;
---- answer
library ieee;
use ieee.std_logic_1164.all;

entity nand_gate is
  port(
    a : in  std_logic;
    b : in  std_logic;
    y : out std_logic
  );
end nand_gate;

architecture rtl of nand_gate is
begin
    y <= not (a and b);
end rtl;
---- hint1
Write the AND in brackets, and put `not` in front of the brackets.
---- hint2
`y <= not (a and b);`
---- solution
`y <= not (a and b);` is an AND gate followed by a NOT gate, as you drew it: the drawing on the right shows both gates.
---- done
VHDL also has the word `nand`: `y <= a nand b;` is the same circuit. Most designers write the gates out like you did, because it reads more clearly.
